---
title: "Theorems and proofs"
---

Mathematical writing needs more than typeset math: it needs *environments*, the
numbered, labelled, cross-referenceable boxes that carry a statement and, after it, a
proof. Taliesin ships eight of them as fenced divs. They are numbered server-side, so
the first paint is already correct, there is no LaTeX package to load and nothing to
re-run when the theme flips.

::: {.theorem #thm-lagrange title="Lagrange"}
For a finite group $G$ and a subgroup $H \le G$, the order $|H|$ divides $|G|$.
:::

::: {.proof}
The left cosets of $H$ partition $G$ and each one has $|H|$ elements, so
$|G| = [G:H]\,|H|$.
:::

That is the entire surface: a `::: {.theorem}` fence, an optional `#thm-…` id, an
optional `title=`, and a `::: {.proof}` after it.

```markdown
::: {.theorem #thm-lagrange title="Lagrange"}
For a finite group $G$ and a subgroup $H \le G$, the order $|H|$ divides $|G|$.
:::

::: {.proof}
The left cosets of $H$ partition $G$ and each one has $|H|$ elements.
:::
```

## The eight environments

The class name *is* the vocabulary: a fenced div whose class is one of these eight
becomes an environment. Each kind counts on its own and carries its own
cross-reference prefix.

| Fence | Renders as | Style | Reference prefix |
|---|---|---|---|
| `::: {.theorem}` | Theorem | plain | `@thm-` |
| `::: {.lemma}` | Lemma | plain | `@lem-` |
| `::: {.corollary}` | Corollary | plain | `@cor-` |
| `::: {.proposition}` | Proposition | plain | `@prp-` |
| `::: {.definition}` | Definition | definition | `@def-` |
| `::: {.example}` | Example | definition | `@exm-` |
| `::: {.remark}` | Remark | remark | `@rem-` |
| `::: {.proof}` | Proof | proof | none (see [Proofs](#proofs)) |

The three styles follow the amsthm convention: **plain** sets the body in italics (the
voice of a claim), while **definition** and **remark** leave it upright (the voice of
a stipulation or an aside). Each style also gets its own accent on the left border, so
the three read apart at a glance without a legend:

::: {.definition #def-group}
A **group** is a set $G$ with an associative binary operation, an identity element,
and an inverse for every element.
:::

::: {.remark}
A definition is upright and a theorem is italic. That is the amsthm distinction, not a
Taliesin invention, and the accent colours are theme variables, so they track light,
dark, and sepia.
:::

There is no namespace prefix on these classes (a callout is `.callout-note`, but a
theorem is just `.theorem`), so this list is also the dispatch table. A misspelled kind
has no prefix to anchor a "did you mean", so `::: {.therom}` quietly falls through and
renders as a plain div. If an environment comes out unstyled and unnumbered, check the
spelling of the class first.

## Titles

A `title=` attribute names the result, rendered parenthetically after the number, the
way `\begin{theorem}[Lagrange]` does:

::: {.proposition title="Intersection of subgroups"}
The intersection of two subgroups of $G$ is again a subgroup.
:::

## Cross-references

Give an environment an id and `@`-reference it anywhere, including from another
chapter. The reference resolves to its label and its number, linked: by @thm-lagrange,
a subgroup's order divides the group's, and @def-group is what "group" means here.

The id must carry the kind's reference prefix from the table above. This is the one
sharp edge worth knowing: an environment is numbered by its **class**, so
`::: {.theorem #pythagoras}` renders as a perfectly good numbered theorem, and yet
`@pythagoras` resolves to nothing, because `pythagoras` names no reference kind.
Rather than leave that silently broken, Taliesin warns and names the fix:

```text
theorem id “pythagoras” cannot be cross-referenced (`@pythagoras` won't resolve); use `thm-pythagoras`
```

So prefix every id you intend to reference (`#thm-pythagoras`, `#lem-coset`,
`#def-group`), and `taliesin check` will tell you when you forget.

## Proofs

`::: {.proof}` is deliberately the odd one out: it is **unnumbered and not
cross-referenceable**, matching the usual convention that a proof is not a numbered
object but an attachment to one. It closes itself with a QED mark, which is decorative
and hidden from screen readers rather than announced as a stray character.

Its `title=` replaces the whole head, which is how you attach a proof to a statement it
does not immediately follow:

::: {.proof title="Proof of Lagrange's theorem"}
Distinct left cosets are disjoint and equinumerous with $H$, so they partition $G$ into
$[G:H]$ blocks of size $|H|$.
:::

A long proof can be folded away with `collapse=`, which is a native `<details>`
disclosure, not a script: it works with JavaScript off, and the browser's own find-in-page
opens it.

::: {.proof collapse="true"}
`collapse="true"` starts the proof closed, so the reader sees only the "Proof." line
until they want the argument. Use `collapse="false"` when you want the fold to exist but
start open.
:::

## Numbering

Numbers run **per kind, in document order**. Nesting does not change that: a theorem
inside a margin aside or a column layout still takes the next number where it was
written, so the reading order and the numbering agree.

The scoping rule is short, and it is the part most likely to surprise anyone arriving
from LaTeX or Quarto:

> A numbered book chapter scopes the number. There is nothing to configure.

Inside a numbered chapter of a book, an environment numbers as `chapter.n`
("Theorem 2.3"). Anywhere else, a standalone page, a website page, a book's `index`
preface, or a chapter whose heading is marked `{.unnumbered}`, there is no chapter to
scope to, so numbering is flat ("Theorem 3").

You are looking at the rule right now. This page is a numbered chapter of the User
Guide book, which is why @thm-lagrange above reads with a chapter prefix rather than as
a bare "Theorem 1". Copy the same fence into a standalone `.tmd` and it renders flat.

This is the same helper every float already uses, which is the point: figures, tables,
listings, equations, and theorems all scope together, so a chapter can never show
"Figure 2.3" beside "Theorem 5". There is no opt-in because there is no coherent way to
opt out: being in a numbered chapter is a fact the renderer already knows, not a
preference. If you are carrying over a `theorems: number-within: chapter` from Quarto,
delete it; the key does not exist, and `check` will flag it as an unknown `theorems`
key rather than ignoring it.

## The `theorems:` block

Two front-matter keys tune numbering per document. Both are optional and the defaults
are what most documents want.

### `shared:` (one counter across kinds)

By default each kind counts independently, so a page can hold Theorem 1 and Lemma 1.
Many mathematicians prefer a single sequence across the claim-like kinds, so the reader
never has to remember which counter a number came from (this is amsthm's
`\newtheorem{lem}[thm]{Lemma}`). List the kinds that should share:

```yaml
---
theorems:
  shared: [theorem, lemma, corollary, proposition]
---
```

Those four now draw from one counter: Theorem 1, Lemma 2, Corollary 3. Only the
*number* is shared, the visible label stays per-kind, and any kind you leave off the
list (`definition` here) keeps counting on its own.

Note that the kind names inside `shared:` are not spell-checked, so a typo does not
warn: the misspelled entry simply never joins the group and that kind keeps its own
counter. If a shared sequence is not merging, check the spelling in the list.

### `numbered:` (whether to number at all)

| Value | Effect |
|---|---|
| `true` | Number everything. The default; you never need to write it |
| `false` | Number nothing. Every environment shows a bare label |
| `unless-unique` | Number a kind only when it occurs more than once |

`unless-unique` is the interesting one. A post with a single definition in it reads
better as "Definition" than "Definition 1", since there is no second one to
distinguish it from, but the moment a second appears the numbers are needed again.
Rather than making you notice and switch, the mode counts occurrences per counter and
decides per kind:

```yaml
---
theorems:
  numbered: unless-unique
---
```

An unnumbered environment is not registered as a reference target, which follows: there
is no number for `@def-x` to resolve to. `numbered:` accepts only those three values,
and anything else warns with a suggestion rather than passing a green check, because a
plausible-looking `numbered: never` would otherwise silently render the exact opposite
of its intent.

## Reading affordances

Environments are wired into the same reader tooling as the rest of a page, with nothing
to configure:

- **Hover a reference** and its statement appears in a preview card, so a reader can
  check what @def-group says without losing their place. It works across chapters too
  (the snippet is served from an index built at discovery), and it is keyboard-reachable:
  focusing the link surfaces the same card.
- **Hover the box itself** and a `#` appears in its head, which copies a deep link
  straight to that theorem.
- **Search** finds environments by their text through the Cmd-K palette, like any other
  block.
